  "text": "We are given:\n\n- $ a $ is divisible by 11,\n- $ b $ is divisible by 15,\n- $ c $ is divisible by 24,\n- $ a + b + c = 504 $,\n- Find $ a^2 + b^2 + c^2 \\mod 11 $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use modular arithmetic to simplify\n\nSince $ a \\equiv 0 \\mod 11 $, we know that:\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\mod 11 \\Rightarrow a^2 \\equiv 0^2 = 0 \\mod 11\n$$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n$$\nb \\equiv 0 \\mod 15 \\Rightarrow b^2 \\equiv 0 \\mod 15 \\Rightarrow b^2 \\equiv 0 \\mod 11 \\Rightarrow b^2 \\equiv 0 \\mod 11\n$$\n\n$$\nc \\equiv 0 \\mod 24 \\Rightarrow c^2 \\equiv 0 \\mod 24 \\Rightarrow c^2 \\equiv 0 \\mod 11\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 \\equiv 0 + 0 + 0 = 0 \\mod 11\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
